% 48c-self-hosting-strings.weft — Stage 8d: the string/memory primitives,
% written in C, compiled by kcc itself, and linked back into the compiler.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: The String Primitives in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-strings}

The arena and the vector proved \kcc{} can compile its own storage and its own
workhorse container; this chapter ports the layer beneath both---the five
string and memory primitives of Chapter~\ref{ch:string-operations}:
\texttt{kcc\_strlen}, \texttt{kcc\_memcpy}, \texttt{kcc\_memcmp},
\texttt{kcc\_memset}, and \texttt{kcc\_strcmp}.  These are the most
\emph{pervasive} functions in the compiler.  \texttt{memcpy} moves every
struct copy and every vector reallocation; \texttt{memset} clears every zeroed
buffer; \texttt{strcmp} and \texttt{strlen} drive keyword recognition, symbol
lookup, and option parsing; \texttt{memcmp} compares interned strings.  Swapping
the hand-written assembly for a C re-implementation compiled by \kcc{}
therefore routes essentially every byte the compiler touches through code
\kcc{} produced.  Because they are leaf routines with no dependencies, the port
is self-contained---no \texttt{extern} declarations at all.

\section{The Byte-Exact Contract}

A drop-in replacement must be behaviourally identical to the last bit, and the
one place that bites is the comparison result.  The assembly loads each byte
with \textsc{ldrb}, which zero-extends, and returns the difference of the two
\emph{unsigned} byte values (\texttt{s1[i]\,-\,s2[i]}, each in $0$--$255$).  A
naive C port using plain \texttt{char}---which is \emph{signed} on this
target---would compare a high-bit byte as negative and return the wrong sign.
The port therefore reads bytes through \texttt{unsigned char} throughout, so
\texttt{kcc\_strcmp} and \texttt{kcc\_memcmp} reproduce the assembly's exact
signed-\texttt{int} result, high bytes and all.

\section{Length}

\texttt{kcc\_strlen} scans to the terminating NUL and returns the count of
bytes before it.

@l c
@d Selfhost: strings strlen @{
unsigned long kcc_strlen(const char *s) {
    const char *p = s;
    while (*p) p = p + 1;
    return (unsigned long)(p - s);
}
@}

\section{Copy and Fill}

\texttt{kcc\_memcpy} copies \texttt{n} bytes front to back (no overlap
handling, matching \texttt{memcpy} rather than \texttt{memmove}) and returns
the destination; \texttt{kcc\_memset} writes the low eight bits of \texttt{c}
into \texttt{n} bytes and returns its buffer.

@l c
@d Selfhost: strings memcpy @{
void *kcc_memcpy(void *dst, const void *src, unsigned long n) {
    unsigned char *d = (unsigned char *)dst;
    const unsigned char *s = (const unsigned char *)src;
    unsigned long i = 0;
    while (i < n) { d[i] = s[i]; i = i + 1; }
    return dst;
}
@}

@l c
@d Selfhost: strings memset @{
void *kcc_memset(void *s, int c, unsigned long n) {
    unsigned char *p = (unsigned char *)s;
    unsigned char b = (unsigned char)c;
    unsigned long i = 0;
    while (i < n) { p[i] = b; i = i + 1; }
    return s;
}
@}

\section{Comparison}

Both comparisons read bytes as \texttt{unsigned char} and return the signed
difference of the first differing pair, zero when the ranges (or strings)
match.  \texttt{kcc\_memcmp} runs to a fixed length; \texttt{kcc\_strcmp}
stops at the first NUL common to both.

@l c
@d Selfhost: strings memcmp @{
int kcc_memcmp(const void *s1, const void *s2, unsigned long n) {
    const unsigned char *a = (const unsigned char *)s1;
    const unsigned char *b = (const unsigned char *)s2;
    unsigned long i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return (int)a[i] - (int)b[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
@}

@l c
@d Selfhost: strings strcmp @{
int kcc_strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2) {
    const unsigned char *a = (const unsigned char *)s1;
    const unsigned char *b = (const unsigned char *)s2;
    while (*a == *b) {
        if (*a == 0) return 0;
        a = a + 1;
        b = b + 1;
    }
    return (int)*a - (int)*b;
}
@}

\section{Assembling the Translation Unit}

The five fragments compose into \texttt{gen/selfhost/strings.c}, routed by
\texttt{assembly.weft}.

@l c
@d Selfhost: strings @{
@<Selfhost: strings strlen@>
@<Selfhost: strings memcpy@>
@<Selfhost: strings memset@>
@<Selfhost: strings memcmp@>
@<Selfhost: strings strcmp@>
@}

\section{Verification}
\label{sec:selfhost-strings-verify}

\texttt{make selfhost-strings} runs the drop-in loop: compile
\texttt{gen/selfhost/strings.c} with the assembly-built \kcc{}, relink a second
compiler with the hand-written \texttt{strings.o} replaced by the freshly
compiled \texttt{strings\_c.o}, and re-run the code-generation suite through it.
Because these primitives underlie every allocation, copy, and name comparison
in the compiler, a green run is the strongest of the self-hosting checks: it
means \kcc{} compiled the very memory operations it then used to compile the
whole suite, byte for byte.

@d Selfhost: strings verify @{
#!/usr/bin/env bash
BASE="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
# make selfhost-strings — compile kcc's string primitives in C with kcc,
# relink, run the suite.
set -euo pipefail

SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)
SH=$BASE/selfhost
sources="main syscalls arena strings io hashtab vec strtab source lexer \
lexer_tables diag preprocessor parser expressions statements declarations \
types symtab conversions sema ir opt emit_arm64 emit_x86 linker codegen emit_macho dwarf driver"

echo "[1/3] compiling $BASE/selfhost/strings.c with kcc"
$BASE/kcc -c "$SH/strings.c" -o "$SH/strings_c.o" >/dev/null

echo "[2/3] linking $BASE/kcc_selfhost_strings (strings.o -> kcc-compiled strings_c.o)"
OBJ=""
for f in $sources; do
    if [ "$f" = "strings" ]; then OBJ="$OBJ $SH/strings_c.o"; else OBJ="$OBJ $BASE/src/${f}.o"; fi
done
ld -o $BASE/kcc_selfhost_strings $OBJ -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64

echo "[3/3] running the codegen suite with the self-hosted string primitives"
cp $BASE/kcc "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_strings"
cp $BASE/kcc_selfhost_strings $BASE/kcc
set +e
bash $BASE/tests/test_codegen.sh
status=$?
cp "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_strings" $BASE/kcc
exit $status
@}
